<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle</groupId>
    <artifactId>eyeandsickle-parent</artifactId>
    <version>${revision}</version>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>eyeandsickle-client</artifactId>
  <name>The Eye and Sickle :: Client</name>
  <description>
    The cross-platform JavaFX desktop client (docs/architecture/01 §1).

    Multi-window by design: one javafx.stage.Stage per tool — map, terminal, rig
    monitor, recon — so the player's screen ends up looking like an operator's
    desk (docs/design/05 §5). A single-window docked fallback is REQUIRED for
    accessibility, not optional (docs/architecture/01 §1, flagged for review).

    The client is a view and input layer. It is never authoritative over
    anything a cheater would forge (Invariant I14). It may mirror server state
    for prediction and it may re-verify provenance chains offline against DID
    public keys (docs/architecture/04 §6.2) — but it decides nothing.
  </description>

  <properties>
    <client.main.class>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle.client.Launcher</client.main.class>
    <javafx.app.class>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle.client.EyeAndSickleClient</javafx.app.class>
    <!--
      What the rig monitor's ABOUT tab reports as the client version. Defaults to the POM version
      and is overridable (-Dclient.version=0.2.0) because a release is named after its git tag
      while the POM stays wherever it was — see .github/workflows/build.yml, which renames the
      artefacts for exactly that reason. Reaches the code through build.properties.
    -->
    <client.version>${project.version}</client.version>
    <!--
      The Discord application id for rich presence (client/presence/RichPresence).

      ⚠ DELIBERATELY EMPTY, and empty is a supported state rather than a missing step. The id is
      public — it identifies the game to Discord and is not a credential — but it belongs to whoever
      registered the application at https://discord.com/developers, so a fork or a contributor's
      local build has no business carrying ours. With this blank, the Settings switch is disabled and
      explains why, nothing opens a pipe, and every test still passes.

      Fill it in here for a real build, or pass -Ddiscord.app.id=… on the command line. It reaches
      the code through build.properties, which is the one filtered resource (see <resources> below);
      -Deyeandsickle.discord.appId=… overrides it at run time without a rebuild.
    -->
    <discord.app.id>1534704782927724584</discord.app.id>
  </properties>

  <dependencies>
    <!--
      ⚠ THE ONLY SPRING ARTIFACT THE CLIENT MAY HAVE, and the enforcer above is shaped to keep it
      that way. It supplies the event broker — ApplicationEventPublisher and the multicaster behind
      it — and nothing else. See client/events/EventBus for what it is used for and
      docs/design/15 §3 for the amendment that permitted it.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-context</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle</groupId>
      <artifactId>eyeandsickle-protocol</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      The single-player runtime. This is what lets the game start with no
      internet, no account and no PostgreSQL.

      It is NOT a back door around the enforcer rules below. `solo` is a plain
      library with no Spring, no driver and no HTTP stack (its own pom enforces
      that), and a character it creates is local-only and can never federate —
      so nothing it produces can enter the economy I14 protects. See
      solo/pom.xml for the full argument.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle</groupId>
      <artifactId>eyeandsickle-engine</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <!--
      solo's TEST jar, for `save/TestSaves` — the shared in-memory SaveStore
      fixture. One fixture rather than one per module; see solo/pom.xml.
      ⚠ Test scope, so it is on no shipped classpath.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle</groupId>
      <artifactId>eyeandsickle-engine</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
      <type>test-jar</type>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      JavaFX platform natives resolve automatically: org.openjfx's parent pom
      carries profiles keyed on os.name/os.arch that set ${javafx.platform} to
      mac-aarch64 / mac / linux / linux-aarch64 / win, and each artifact pulls
      its own classifier. No os-maven-plugin needed for local development.
      Building for a different OS than the one you are on means pinning the
      classifier explicitly; see the packaging note at the bottom of this file.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.openjfx</groupId>
      <artifactId>javafx-controls</artifactId>
      <version>${javafx.version}</version>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.openjfx</groupId>
      <artifactId>javafx-fxml</artifactId>
      <version>${javafx.version}</version>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Native-feeling theming across macOS / Linux / Windows so the client
         doesn't read as a 2005 Swing app on any of them. -->
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <!--
      ⚠ TWO RESOURCE ENTRIES OVER ONE DIRECTORY, AND THE SPLIT IS LOAD-BEARING. Filtering the
      whole of src/main/resources would rewrite the two bundled TTFs and the mascot PNG byte for
      byte — Maven's filter is a text transform and does not know it is looking at a font — and
      would eat any `${...}` a term page happens to contain. So exactly one file is filtered, by
      name, and everything else is copied verbatim. Losing the <excludes> below would produce a
      build that succeeds and a client whose fonts no longer load.
    -->
    <resources>
      <resource>
        <directory>src/main/resources</directory>
        <filtering>false</filtering>
        <excludes>
          <exclude>io/github/stoicswe/eyeandsickle/client/build.properties</exclude>
        </excludes>
      </resource>
      <resource>
        <directory>src/main/resources</directory>
        <filtering>true</filtering>
        <includes>
          <include>io/github/stoicswe/eyeandsickle/client/build.properties</include>
        </includes>
      </resource>
    </resources>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.openjfx</groupId>
        <artifactId>javafx-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <mainClass>${javafx.app.class}</mainClass>
          <options>
            <!--
              JavaFX loads native graphics libraries via System::load, which JDK
              24+ reports as a restricted-method call and which a future JDK will
              block outright. Granting it explicitly silences the warning now and
              stops the client from breaking on a JDK bump later.
            -->
            <option>--enable-native-access=javafx.graphics</option>
            <!-- What macOS puts in the menu bar and the dock. Without it the running application is
                 called "java", which is the runtime's name rather than the game's. Harmless on
                 Windows and Linux, which ignore the flag. Launcher sets the matching system
                 properties for the platforms that read those instead.

                 ⚠ THE INNER QUOTES ARE LOad-BEARING AND THIS BROKE javafx:run FOR A WHILE. The
                 plugin hands its options to commons-exec, which TOKENISES ON WHITESPACE — so the
                 unquoted form arrived as three arguments and the second of them, `uOS`, was taken
                 as the main class:

                     Error: Could not find or load main class uOS

                 A message that names neither the option nor the plugin, on the one command
                 CLAUDE.md documents as the way to launch the client. Any option value here that
                 contains a space needs the same treatment. -->
            <option>-Xdock:name="EAS uOS Client"</option>
          </options>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>

      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-jar-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <archive>
            <manifest>
              <mainClass>${client.main.class}</mainClass>
            </manifest>
          </archive>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>

      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-enforcer-plugin</artifactId>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>client-is-not-authoritative</id>
            <goals><goal>enforce</goal></goals>
            <configuration>
              <rules>
                <bannedDependencies>
                  <message>
                    INVARIANT I14. The client must not depend on the server
                    module, must never reach a database driver, and must not
                    acquire the parts of Spring that talk to either. Server code
                    on the client classpath is how "the client decided the
                    outcome" bugs start. Talk to the server over REST/WebSocket
                    using types from eyeandsickle-protocol.

                    AMENDED 2026-07-29: spring-context (with core, beans and aop)
                    is PERMITTED, for the in-process event broker and nothing
                    else — see client/events/EventBus and docs/design/15 UI-7.
                    An event multicaster does not make the client authoritative,
                    which is the thing this rule protects.

                    AMENDED 2026-08-03: H2, Flyway and spring-jdbc are PERMITTED,
                    transitively through eyeandsickle-engine, because single player
                    now keeps its character in a local database written by the
                    SAME store a home server uses. One save system, one migration,
                    one set of bytes.

                    ⚠ RE-READ WHAT THIS RULE IS FOR BEFORE WIDENING IT AGAIN. It
                    is not "the client must not touch a database" — the client has
                    always hosted the rules engine in process for single player,
                    so a driver changes nothing about who decides. It is that
                    SERVER CODE MUST NOT REACH THE CLIENT: the federation logic,
                    the ledger, the quorum, the authority tables. That is why
                    eyeandsickle-server, spring-web and all of Spring Boot stay
                    banned, and why the ban on those is the half that matters.

                    ⚠ AND A LOCAL DATABASE IS NOT A TRUST BOUNDARY. A solo save is
                    as editable in H2 as it was in JSON. What keeps a solo
                    character harmless is the quarantine rule (docs/architecture/12
                    §1) — it can never federate — and never the storage format.

                    The absence of a &lt;dependency&gt; element is not
                    self-defending — someone will add one. This rule is. Adding
                    a Spring module NOT on the permitted list is the drift this
                    amendment is shaped to keep catching.
                  </message>
                  <searchTransitive>true</searchTransitive>
                  <excludes>
                    <exclude>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle:eyeandsickle-server</exclude>
                    <!--
                      Everything under org.springframework EXCEPT the four the
                      event broker needs. Listed as a broad ban plus explicit
                      includes rather than as a list of banned modules: a new
                      Spring module published tomorrow is caught by the first
                      form and invisible to the second.
                    -->
                    <exclude>org.springframework:*</exclude>
                    <exclude>org.springframework.boot:*</exclude>
                    <exclude>org.postgresql:*</exclude>
                    <exclude>org.hibernate*:*</exclude>
                  </excludes>
                  <includes>
                    <include>org.springframework:spring-context</include>
                    <include>org.springframework:spring-core</include>
                    <include>org.springframework:spring-beans</include>
                    <include>org.springframework:spring-aop</include>
                    <include>org.springframework:spring-expression</include>
                    <include>org.springframework:spring-jcl</include>
                    <!-- The save system, via eyeandsickle-engine. See the message. -->
                    <include>org.springframework:spring-jdbc</include>
                    <include>org.springframework:spring-tx</include>
                  </includes>
                </bannedDependencies>
              </rules>
            </configuration>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>


  <profiles>
    <!--
      SELF-CONTAINED RUNNABLE JARS, one per platform, all buildable from ONE machine:

          for p in win mac mac-aarch64 linux linux-aarch64; do
            mvn -Pdist -Ddist.platform=$p -pl client -am package -DskipTests
          done

      produces client/target/eyeandsickle-client-<version>-<platform>.jar, each launched with
      `java -jar <file>`. Unlike jpackage this needs no machine of the target OS, because
      nothing is compiled per platform — only a different set of prebuilt JavaFX natives is
      packaged. `dist.platform` defaults to this machine's, so a plain `mvn -Pdist ... package`
      builds the one you can test locally.

      ⚠ WHY NOT ONE JAR FOR EVERYTHING — this was built, measured and rejected, so please do
      not "simplify" it back. JavaFX puts its natives at the JAR ROOT under names that carry
      the OS but NOT the architecture: `libglass.dylib` is the name on both Intel and Apple
      Silicon, and `libglass.so` on both x64 and ARM Linux. Merging all five classifier jars
      therefore has the two Mac builds overwrite each other, and the two Linux builds likewise
      — silently, because shade sees identical paths and keeps the last one. The resulting jar
      really did contain a single x86_64 `libglass.dylib`, and it failed on the arm64 machine
      that built it with:

          UnsatisfiedLinkError ... (have 'x86_64', need 'arm64')

      Windows-x64-plus-Intel-Mac-plus-x64-Linux in one file is achievable and is what that jar
      was. It is not worth having: it excludes every Apple Silicon Mac, which is most of them.

      A true single jar is possible but needs the launcher to extract arch-scoped natives and
      point JavaFX at them through `javafx.cachedir` (whose layout is `<dir>/<runtime version>/
      <os.arch>/`) — internals, undocumented, and silently wrong again if they ever change.
      Per-platform jars cost one loop and depend on nothing.

      ⚠ MUST RUN FROM THE CLASSPATH, which this client already requires: `Launcher` does not
      extend Application precisely so the JVM does not look for JavaFX on the module path before
      main() runs. `java -jar` is a classpath launch, so the two agree rather than fight. Do NOT
      add module-info.java to make this "cleaner"; that recreates the jlink problem below.

      ⚠ DOES NOT bundle a Java runtime — the player needs JDK/JRE 25+ on PATH. That is the
      trade against jpackage, which does bundle one but CANNOT CROSS-COMPILE: a Windows image
      must be built on Windows. This needs one machine; that needs three.

      ⚠ On JDK 24+ a classpath launch warns unless started with the
      `enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED` flag (two leading dashes; XML comments cannot contain
      them). It is a warning, not a failure. The `javafx.graphics` form of that flag is WRONG
      here and grants nothing — see CLAUDE.md; this is the one flag that differs by launch mode.
    -->
    <profile>
      <id>dist</id>
      <properties>
        <!-- org.openjfx's parent pom sets ${javafx.platform} from os.name/os.arch, so the
             default is whatever this machine is and a local `mvn -Pdist` is testable. -->
        <dist.platform>${javafx.platform}</dist.platform>
      </properties>
      <dependencies>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.openjfx</groupId>
        <artifactId>javafx-base</artifactId>
        <version>${javafx.version}</version>
        <classifier>${dist.platform}</classifier>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.openjfx</groupId>
        <artifactId>javafx-graphics</artifactId>
        <version>${javafx.version}</version>
        <classifier>${dist.platform}</classifier>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.openjfx</groupId>
        <artifactId>javafx-controls</artifactId>
        <version>${javafx.version}</version>
        <classifier>${dist.platform}</classifier>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.openjfx</groupId>
        <artifactId>javafx-fxml</artifactId>
        <version>${javafx.version}</version>
        <classifier>${dist.platform}</classifier>
      </dependency>
      </dependencies>
      <build>
        <plugins>
          <plugin>
            <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
            <artifactId>maven-shade-plugin</artifactId>
            <executions>
              <execution>
                <phase>package</phase>
                <goals><goal>shade</goal></goals>
                <configuration>
                  <shadedArtifactAttached>true</shadedArtifactAttached>
                  <shadedClassifierName>${dist.platform}</shadedClassifierName>
                  <createDependencyReducedPom>false</createDependencyReducedPom>
                  <transformers>
                    <transformer implementation="org.apache.maven.plugins.shade.resource.ManifestResourceTransformer">
                      <mainClass>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle.client.Launcher</mainClass>
                      <manifestEntries>
                        <!-- Jackson ships multi-release jars; without this the JDK ignores their
                             versioned classes and Jackson fails in ways that look like ours. -->
                        <Multi-Release>true</Multi-Release>
                      </manifestEntries>
                    </transformer>
                    <!-- ServiceLoader files must be CONCATENATED, not overwritten. Jackson 3
                         discovers modules this way, and last-one-wins would drop every provider
                         but one — surfacing as a save that will not deserialise rather than as
                         anything resembling a packaging error. -->
                    <transformer implementation="org.apache.maven.plugins.shade.resource.ServicesResourceTransformer"/>
                  </transformers>
                  <filters>
                    <filter>
                      <artifact>*:*</artifact>
                      <excludes>
                        <!-- Several copies of the same descriptor cannot coexist, and a classpath
                             launch never reads them anyway. -->
                        <exclude>module-info.class</exclude>
                        <exclude>META-INF/versions/*/module-info.class</exclude>
                        <!-- Signatures do not survive repackaging: the digests stop matching the
                             contents and the JVM refuses the jar with a SecurityException. -->
                        <exclude>META-INF/*.SF</exclude>
                        <exclude>META-INF/*.DSA</exclude>
                        <exclude>META-INF/*.RSA</exclude>
                      </excludes>
                    </filter>
                  </filters>
                </configuration>
              </execution>
            </executions>
          </plugin>
        </plugins>
      </build>
    </profile>
    <!--
      SELF-CONTAINED APPLICATION FOR THE HOST PLATFORM — bundles a Java runtime, so the
      player needs no JDK at all:

          mvn -Pnative -pl client -am package -DskipTests                  # app image
          mvn -Pnative -Djpackage.installer=true -pl client -am package    # native installer

      Output lands in `client/target/jpackage/`:

          macOS    EAS uOS Client.app          →  installer=true gives a .dmg
          Windows  EAS uOS Client\             →  installer=true gives an .msi
          Linux    EAS uOS Client/             →  installer=true gives a .deb

      ⚠ WRITE `installer=true`, NOT A BARE `-Djpackage.installer`. Maven's -D is declared to
      commons-cli as taking two arguments split on `=`, so a bare flag SWALLOWS THE NEXT
      ARGUMENT: `-Djpackage.installer -pl client` sets the property to "-pl" and leaves Maven
      hunting for a reactor project called "client". Measured, and the error names neither the
      property nor the real cause.

      ⚠ HOST PLATFORM ONLY, AND THAT IS JPACKAGE, NOT A CHOICE WE MADE. jpackage embeds the
      runtime of the JDK running the build and emits a platform-native launcher binary, so it
      cannot cross-compile: a Windows .exe launcher must be produced on Windows. Shipping all
      five platforms therefore needs `-Pdist` (or the `client-dist` module), which needs one
      machine but bundles no runtime. The two profiles are complements, not rivals.

      Nothing here has to be told which platform it is on:

        · the JavaFX natives come from the plain, classifier-less `javafx-controls`/`javafx-fxml`
          dependencies above, whose own poms carry os.name/os.arch profiles that pull this
          machine's classifier — the same resolution `mvn test` already relies on. `-Pdist`'s
          explicit `${dist.platform}` classifiers are deliberately NOT used here, precisely
          because host-platform is the whole point of this profile;
        · the runtime is jlinked from the JDK running Maven;
        · the installer type and the OS-specific options come from the three os-activated
          profiles below.

      ⚠ OS-SPECIFIC OPTIONS MUST LIVE IN OS-ACTIVATED PROFILES, never in one shared block.
      jpackage REJECTS a foreign option outright rather than ignoring it — `win-menu` on a Mac
      is `Option [win-menu] is not valid on this platform`, a hard failure — so a single
      configuration listing winMenu + linuxShortcut + macPackageIdentifier cannot build
      anywhere. Verified against jpackage 26.

      ⚠ INPUT IS A DEDICATED DIRECTORY HOLDING EXACTLY ONE JAR. jpackage copies *everything*
      under `input` into the app, and puts every jar it finds there on the classpath (verified:
      the generated .cfg gets one `app.classpath=` line per jar). Pointing it at `target/` would
      ship `classes/`, `test-classes/`, `maven-status/` and the archiver working directories
      inside the application. The shade execution below writes one uber jar into
      `target/jpackage-input/` and nothing else goes there.

      ⚠ MUST RUN FROM THE CLASSPATH — `main-jar`, never `module`. `Launcher` does not extend
      Application so the JVM does not look for JavaFX on the module path before main() runs; see
      the dist profile's note and CLAUDE.md. That is also why `enable-native-access` takes the
      ALL-UNNAMED form here and the `javafx.graphics` form in `javafx:run` — the one flag in this
      build that differs by launch mode, and the module form silently grants nothing.

      ⚠ WHY jlink ALONE STILL DOES NOT WORK, and why that does not block this. jlink can only
      link explicit JPMS modules, and `io.github.erdtman:java-json-canonicalization` is a plain
      Java 8 jar that can only ever be an automatic module. jpackage in NON-MODULAR mode does not
      care: it jlinks a runtime out of the JDK's own modules and puts our classpath jar beside it.
      The cost is image size (the default runtime is generous). Trimming it means a hand-built
      `runtime-image`, which is a real project — full JPMS is option 2 and remains open.
    -->
    <profile>
      <id>native</id>
      <properties>
        <!--
          app-image is the default because it needs NO external tooling: an .msi needs WiX, a
          .deb needs dpkg/fakeroot, an .rpm needs rpm-build. A profile whose documented command
          fails on a clean machine is worse than no profile, so the installer is opt-in
          (-Djpackage.installer=true) and the os profiles below choose the right type for the
          host. Override the type directly with -Djpackage.type=PKG / EXE / RPM — a CLI -D beats
          a profile property.

          ⚠ These are the plugin's ENUM CONSTANT names, not jpackage's spelling: `app-image`
          fails the build with "No enum constant ImageType.app-image" before jpackage ever runs.
        -->
        <jpackage.type>APP_IMAGE</jpackage.type>
        <jpackage.input>${project.build.directory}/jpackage-input</jpackage.input>
        <jpackage.dest>${project.build.directory}/jpackage</jpackage.dest>
        <!--
          ⚠ NOT ${revision}, and this is not laziness. jpackage validates app-version hard:
          `0.1.0-SNAPSHOT` fails with "contains invalid component [0-SNAPSHOT]", and even a clean
          `0.1.0` fails on macOS with "The first number in an app-version cannot be zero" because
          it becomes CFBundleVersion. Both verified. Pass a real one at release time with
          -Djpackage.appVersion=1.2.3; until the project's own version clears 1.0 the two cannot
          be the same string.
        -->
        <jpackage.appVersion>1.0.0</jpackage.appVersion>
      </properties>
      <build>
        <plugins>
          <plugin>
            <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
            <artifactId>maven-shade-plugin</artifactId>
            <executions>
              <execution>
                <id>shade-for-jpackage</id>
                <phase>package</phase>
                <goals><goal>shade</goal></goals>
                <configuration>
                  <!--
                    outputFile writes the uber jar to an explicit path and leaves the module's
                    own artifact alone — so `-Pnative` does not change what gets installed, and
                    the input directory contains this file and nothing else.
                  -->
                  <outputFile>${jpackage.input}/eyeandsickle-client.jar</outputFile>
                  <createDependencyReducedPom>false</createDependencyReducedPom>
                  <transformers>
                    <transformer implementation="org.apache.maven.plugins.shade.resource.ManifestResourceTransformer">
                      <mainClass>${client.main.class}</mainClass>
                      <manifestEntries>
                        <!-- Jackson ships multi-release jars; without this the JDK ignores their
                             versioned classes and Jackson fails in ways that look like ours. -->
                        <Multi-Release>true</Multi-Release>
                      </manifestEntries>
                    </transformer>
                    <!-- ServiceLoader files must be CONCATENATED, not overwritten — see the dist
                         profile; last-one-wins surfaces as a save that will not deserialise. -->
                    <transformer implementation="org.apache.maven.plugins.shade.resource.ServicesResourceTransformer"/>
                  </transformers>
                  <filters>
                    <filter>
                      <artifact>*:*</artifact>
                      <excludes>
                        <exclude>module-info.class</exclude>
                        <exclude>META-INF/versions/*/module-info.class</exclude>
                        <exclude>META-INF/*.SF</exclude>
                        <exclude>META-INF/*.DSA</exclude>
                        <exclude>META-INF/*.RSA</exclude>
                      </excludes>
                    </filter>
                  </filters>
                </configuration>
              </execution>
            </executions>
          </plugin>

          <plugin>
            <groupId>org.panteleyev</groupId>
            <artifactId>jpackage-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            <executions>
              <execution>
                <id>jpackage-host-platform</id>
                <phase>package</phase>
                <goals><goal>jpackage</goal></goals>
              </execution>
            </executions>
            <configuration>
              <type>${jpackage.type}</type>
              <name>EAS uOS Client</name>
              <appVersion>${jpackage.appVersion}</appVersion>
              <vendor>stoicswe</vendor>
              <copyright>The Eye and Sickle</copyright>
              <description>The Eye and Sickle — uOS client</description>

              <input>${jpackage.input}</input>
              <mainJar>eyeandsickle-client.jar</mainJar>
              <mainClass>${client.main.class}</mainClass>
              <destination>${jpackage.dest}</destination>

              <javaOptions>
                <!--
                  ⚠ Two leading dashes, and they are legal here: the "no double hyphen" rule is
                  an XML *comment* restriction, not an element-content one. Dropping them gives
                  the bundled JVM `enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED`, which it rejects as an
                  unrecognised option — the app then fails to start with no clue why.
                -->
                <option>--enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED</option>
              </javaOptions>

              <!--
                No icon yet: jpackage errors if the path does not exist, so an <icon> element
                pointing at a placeholder would break the build. When there is artwork, add
                src/main/jpackage/{macos/icon.icns,windows/icon.ico,linux/icon.png} and put the
                <icon> element in the matching os profile below — the formats are not
                interchangeable.
              -->
            </configuration>
          </plugin>
        </plugins>
      </build>
    </profile>

    <!--
      Host-platform installer options. Each activates on os.family AND on -Djpackage.installer,
      so a plain `-Pnative` build stays a bare app-image and never trips jpackage's
      "not valid with type [app-image]" check on an installer-only option like win-menu.
      Configuration here merges into the execution declared above.
    -->
    <profile>
      <id>native-installer-mac</id>
      <activation>
        <os><family>mac</family></os>
        <property><name>jpackage.installer</name></property>
      </activation>
      <properties>
        <jpackage.type>DMG</jpackage.type>
      </properties>
      <build>
        <plugins>
          <plugin>
            <groupId>org.panteleyev</groupId>
            <artifactId>jpackage-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            <configuration>
              <macPackageName>EAS uOS Client</macPackageName>
              <macPackageIdentifier>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle.client</macPackageIdentifier>
            </configuration>
          </plugin>
        </plugins>
      </build>
    </profile>

    <profile>
      <id>native-installer-windows</id>
      <activation>
        <os><family>windows</family></os>
        <property><name>jpackage.installer</name></property>
      </activation>
      <properties>
        <jpackage.type>MSI</jpackage.type>
      </properties>
      <build>
        <plugins>
          <plugin>
            <groupId>org.panteleyev</groupId>
            <artifactId>jpackage-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            <configuration>
              <winMenu>true</winMenu>
              <winMenuGroup>The Eye and Sickle</winMenuGroup>
              <winShortcut>true</winShortcut>
              <winDirChooser>true</winDirChooser>
              <!--
                ⚠ A FIXED UUID is what makes the next .msi an UPGRADE rather than a second
                parallel installation. Generate one once and never change it.
              -->
              <winUpgradeUuid>5e6f3f1e-6f2b-4a2b-9a3e-1b7f4c9d2a10</winUpgradeUuid>
            </configuration>
          </plugin>
        </plugins>
      </build>
    </profile>

    <profile>
      <id>native-installer-linux</id>
      <activation>
        <!--
          ⚠ os.name, NOT family "unix" — macOS matches the unix family too, so a family match
          here would fire on a Mac and hand jpackage linux-shortcut, which it refuses.
          Maven's family vocabulary has no "linux" at all.
        -->
        <os><name>Linux</name></os>
        <property><name>jpackage.installer</name></property>
      </activation>
      <properties>
        <jpackage.type>DEB</jpackage.type>
      </properties>
      <build>
        <plugins>
          <plugin>
            <groupId>org.panteleyev</groupId>
            <artifactId>jpackage-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            <configuration>
              <!-- dpkg names must be lowercase and free of spaces; the app's display name is not. -->
              <linuxPackageName>eas-uos-client</linuxPackageName>
              <linuxAppCategory>Game</linuxAppCategory>
              <linuxMenuGroup>Game</linuxMenuGroup>
              <linuxShortcut>true</linuxShortcut>
            </configuration>
          </plugin>
        </plugins>
      </build>
    </profile>
  </profiles>

  <!--
    SELF-CONTAINED PACKAGING — where it stands, so nobody re-derives the dead end.

    docs/architecture/01 §1 lists this as a [PROPOSAL] so players never have to "install a JRE
    first". `-Pnative` above delivers it, in jpackage's NON-MODULAR mode: `type app-image`,
    `input <dir>`, `main-jar`. Host platform only, bundled runtime, no module-info anywhere.

    ⚠ `javafx:jlink` was tried first and cannot work here — a structural limit, not a
    misconfiguration. jlink links only EXPLICIT JPMS modules; this client runs from the
    classpath with no module-info.java, and the provenance canonicalizer
    `io.github.erdtman:java-json-canonicalization` is a plain Java 8 jar that can only ever be
    an AUTOMATIC module, which jlink refuses. jpackage's non-modular mode sidesteps it by
    jlinking a runtime out of the JDK's own modules and putting our jar on the classpath beside
    it — which is why the profile is written that way and must stay that way.

    Still open, and a real dependency-graph project rather than an afternoon: full JPMS
    (module-info.java in protocol and client, plus a modular replacement for the canonicalizer)
    would let the runtime be trimmed to what the client actually uses. Until then the image
    carries the JDK's default module set.
  -->
</project>
